generally thought about where to place the paywall and where not to.

183
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:03,040
And I bring that back to the example I gave you before in swiping right on an unlimited basis.

184
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:05,280
That is not something you want people to be able to do.

185
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:11,440
It turns out, you know, everybody to be able to do because it creates a less effective ecosystem for everyone.

186
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:15,360
But when you install a paywall, it makes people think twice about it.

187
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:19,200
And the people that are really high intent that really want to do this, they pay.

188
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:22,560
And that is, that's like a good paradigm for how to think about it.

189
00:17:22,720 --> 00:17:33,200
So, you know, one of the early product elements that went into kind of Tinder plus was the abilility to break through that limit in the course of a day.

190
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:35,600
Anyway, so that worked very well.

191
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:38,160
The other, I'll give you another example is Passport.

192
00:17:38,560 --> 00:17:43,280
Passport was a product feature created that allowed you to effectively change your location.

193
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:48,400
So, you know, Tinder, as I think everybody knows at this point, is based on a radius of where you are.

194
00:17:48,800 --> 00:17:53,920
You know, your search radius is limited to kind of some proximity to where you are.

195
00:17:54,720 --> 00:18:07,840
If we let everyone in the world change their location and be anywhere, it would mean nobody would know who was actually around them, which would then, I mean, and remember the ultimate.ate goal of Tinder is actually for you to get off the product and meet somebody in real life.

196
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:13,440
So it has a, you know, I think what I consider a very, you know, ideal aim.

197
00:18:13,920 --> 00:18:16,720
Again, doesn't always work out that way, but that's the ideal aim of Tinder.

198
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:20,720
That doesn't work if everybody's spread all over the world and you're matching with people that aren't anywhere near you.

199
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:25,200
So passport became an element of the paywall, which is okay.

200
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:32,400
If somebody actually really wants to do this because they happen to be going on a trip in a few months or and they want to match with people before they get there, which is a very common use case.

201
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:34,320
That's an okay thing to do.

202
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:39,360
It makes total logical sense to charge for because if everybody, if we gave this to everybody and let everybody do it anytime.

203
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:50,160
If we had a percentage of people doing it, it allowed us to generate revenue and flesh out where people had real intent.

204
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:52,800
So those are a couple of examples of how we thought about it.

205
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:57,520
And I think the paradigm of how we thought about what to place behind the paywall and what to place in front of it.

206
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:58,800
It's really interesting.

207
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:04,560
And when you have a system like Tinder, which you describe as a game, I could think it's quite literally, right?

208
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:07,760
Like in some, like I'm sure there's a way to represent it as game theory.

209
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:08,640
And there's no question.

210
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:10,400
It's just, it just matters a lot to people.

211
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:14,800
So I'm very conscious of not minimizing, like, it's all a game,, you know?

212
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:16,320
Right, exactly, exactly.

213
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:23,600
It's very different from a lot of the more personal, more isolated mobile subscription apps that are generally based on content, right?

214
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:26,000
Like it's a different, it's a whole other dimension of thinking, right?

215
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:28,080
And actually, maybe in some ways simplifies it, right?

216
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:43,600
Because you can kind of decide, like, what are behaviors we need to discourage or put behind a paywall and like you're controlling an ecosystem in some ways or managing an ecosystem versus, you know, some content-based meditation app or something like that, along those lines where it's a different game.

217
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:48,640
So if you have something like that that actually maybe in some ways more complicated but does like.

218
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:59,040
like, uh, give you a little bit of guidance in terms of like, how can we, yeah, how can we, how can we use monetization to control, to control behavior, which is like, that's what the government does, right?

219
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:00,480
Like, yeah, no question.

220
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:03,920
I mean, it's just, it's just straight economic theory at the end of the day.

221
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:08,480
And look, I think if you're running a, and this is, I guess maybe I'll put my investor hat on for a minute.

222
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:20,080
If you're running a content-based subscription business, then like, listen, it's just, it's so much more about, you know, understanding your customer and just raw optimization, right?

223
00:20:20,240 --> 00:20:22,800
I mean, that's like, that is just the science around it.

224
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:23,440
We, you know.

225
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,160
And by the way, there's like many layers to that.

226
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:30,480
We're an investor in a company named Brilliant, brilliant.org.

227
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:34,560
The founder, Sue, to I guess use the word brilliant founder.

228
00:20:34,880 --> 00:20:36,880
And this is not even one I would take credit for.

229
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:39,440
My partner, Sam, has known Sue for decades at this point.

230
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:42,000
But they are a STEM subscription.

231
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:47,840
They're a STEM subscription or education platform that monetizes through subscription.

232
00:20:48,720 --> 00:20:50,800
And they have done a brilliant job.

233
00:20:51,360 --> 00:21:12,080
to use the word one more time, of understanding not just how to place things inside and outside of a paywall so they can take the dollars and reinvest in great content for their users, but also to figure out how best to optimize for the user's preference on a subscription period, annual versus quarterly versus monthly, which is a science unto itself.

234
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:28,000
So there are, and that's just a pure content monetization business, but I think that one is really just about the product that you have, why people are going to engage, like the frequency with which people are going to engage with that product, why they're going to come back to you and how they would prefer to.

235
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:29,200
pay you for that.

236
00:21:29,600 --> 00:21:31,040
You know, it's really nothing more than that.

237
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:37,760
So I think you just need raw analytics and like rapid experimentation to understand, you know, where you fall in that cycle.

238
00:21:37,840 --> 00:21:42,560
And you can obviously borrow ideas from other companies, but, you know, a lot of it has to do with just rapid experimentation.

239
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:49,120
You're probably less likely to get really fun and interesting unexpected side effects as well, like you would with an environment like Tinder, right?

240
00:21:49,520 --> 00:21:51,040
Like you can't change one little thing.

241
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:52,000
The ripple effects.

242
00:21:52,160 --> 00:21:55,440
Yeah, the ripple effect is much, is much more limited, thankfully.

243
00:21:55,840 --> 00:21:55,920
Yeah.

244
00:21:56,640 --> 00:21:56,720
Yeah.

245
00:21:56,960 --> 00:21:58,800
That's very true, for better or worse, I suppose.

246
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:05,520
In all that thinking about your customer and the, and the monetization strategy, your role was CMO.

247
00:22:05,840 --> 00:22:13,040
And one of the things Tinder did really well was to align that user acquisition strategy to that monetization strategy.

248
00:22:13,440 --> 00:22:15,920
So what, yeah, what was your thinking as CMO?

249
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:19,840
Like, a lot of CMOs aren't quite as deep into the weeds at all this.

250
00:22:20,320 --> 00:22:28,800
So it kind of speaks to how you, how you all operated that you were so in the weeds on the monetization side, but your role was user acquisition.

251
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:34,960
So how did, how did you blend those two and focus acquisition around that monetization strategy?

252
00:22:35,920 --> 00:22:36,320
Great question.

253
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:46,400
So what was interesting, I think, about stepping into Tinder when it was just hitting its inflection point was we remember we were free.

254
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:49,040
We had no monetization at all for a long period of time.

255
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:51,760
And as a function of that, we were very careful on budgeting.

256
00:22:51,920 --> 00:23:13,920
Like we, the team collectively did a masterful job of organizing around very efficient channels of awareness, meaning we understood very quickly that Tinder, because it was a dating product and was mobile and there was this sense that everybody was on it or everybody was going to be on it and there was FOMO around not being on it.

257
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:29,680
We understood very quickly that the press would cover a variety of things about the company or about the product and not every single day, not a not a sort of daily drum beat, but if we could create sort of a periodic drum beat of information that we could stay in the media.

258
00:23:29,920 --> 00:23:39,280
And we had looked, by the way, to OKCupid before it, which had, you know, a freemium product and therefore a limited marketing budget and figured out how through very data driven blogs.

259
00:23:39,920 --> 00:23:41,520
I think they invented that actually, didn't they?

260
00:23:41,600 --> 00:23:43,440
Like, I don't know of anyone who did it before them.

261
00:23:43,680 --> 00:23:44,880
No, no, I think that's right.

262
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:53,440
And they wrote a very data driven blog that the press and oftentimes the results would be controversial and they said, we're just going to publish the results and the press would pick that up and write about it.

263
00:23:53,600 --> 00:24:03,360
It some would say, look at the look at this craziness that okay cupid is is you know, you know, they okay cupid did a blog on whether its own users were racist based on the data as an example.

264
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:04,640
So like very controversial.

265
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:06,640
I think that's when I remember being exposed to it.

266
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:06,880
Right.

267
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:07,520
Exactly.

268
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:08,160
Very controversial.

269
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:20,240
So we so we sort of knew that we had learned from that playbook or observed that playbook and we sort of knew that if we and then and that exact approach didn't make sense for Tinder, but there were approaches that did and we figured out how to kind of leverage media.

270
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:29,840
on a global basis actually just because there is a bit of an echo chamber in the world of media that if you know one writes a story it can get picked up and moved out.

271
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:35,280
And we very quickly understood, I think, how to handle that, both the marketing and the PR teams.

272
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:37,360
And so we would go down that road.

273
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:49,040
And then I think, you know, when it came to like connecting monetization to marketing, when we introduced super like as an example, which is a, uh, a paid feature that allows you, again, it's fascinating.

274
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:55,120
Like, I think a brilliantly conceived product feature that allows you to set, you know, by default gives you gave users one super like a day.

275
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:58,000
So I can swipe left and right as I would like throughout the day.

276
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:01,040
I get one super like a day by default to send to somebody.

277
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:04,000
And that has, again, these, these sort of.

278
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:13,840
the consequence as long as people understand it of the recipient knowing that okay somebody used their one a day super like on me and it moves you to the top of the stack and all these things.

279
00:25:14,080 --> 00:25:20,880
And so again, a brilliantly conceived product, but the key was that the recipient needed to understand that there was only one a day.

280
00:25:21,360 --> 00:25:38,880
And so we actually created both a marketing campaign around it, but also that went outside of the Tinder product, but also we actually effectively created a branded profile that we could introduce into the product that would market the product features that gave marketing basically a connection into the actual.

281
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:40,800
product, which we didn't really have before.

282
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:49,360
I mean, it all sounds like easy in hindsight, but the reality is like when I got to Tinder, because things had happened so quickly, there was very little data.

283
00:25:50,320 --> 00:25:53,280
There like we didn't have a good organization of data.

284
00:25:53,520 --> 00:25:55,920
We didn't really have hooks between marketing and product at all.

285
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:58,000
So, and we didn't have life cycle marketing at all.

286
00:25:58,160 --> 00:25:59,440
We didn't have CRM at all.

287
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:02,640
I mean, other than like something hardcoded somewhere in the product.

288
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:04,480
So basically told you when you got a match.

289
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:05,440
That was like it.

290
00:26:06,160 --> 00:26:10,640
And so we had to do like, you know, we'd hire, we'd do a lot of work to get those things in line.

291
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:20,480
And when it came to introducing super like, we'd leverage those things to get the message out to users that then drove the recipient to understand this is a once a day thing.

292
00:26:20,800 --> 00:26:21,440
It made it special.

293
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:31,040
And as long as they understood it, it would perform for the users that sent the super like, meaning their match rate would go up as a function of sending a super like.

294
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:40,000
And that would drive then further monetization because we introduced super like as an a la carte feature on top of our subscription eventually that allowed you to buy more super likes.

295
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:51,280
And so all of those things got connected and all of those things worked very, very closely with one another, but it all started it all required education of the users to understand it both on the sender and the recipient side of the equation.

296
00:26:51,760 --> 00:26:51,840
Wow.

297
00:26:52,480 --> 00:26:52,560
Yeah.

298
00:26:53,920 --> 00:26:56,480
Tinder's monetization has always been so fascinating to me.

299
00:26:56,720 --> 00:27:08,320
And it's all starting to make sense now when you think about it from that kind of game theory perspective of like super like is breaking the rules and that makes sense why that's a paid feature.

300
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:11,200
The profile boost is kind of breaking the rules.

301
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:12,880
You get more attention than you otherwise would.

302
00:27:13,120 --> 00:27:14,000
That's a paid feature.

303
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:26,000
And I haven't looked at the paywall recently, but but yeah, framing in that context is just so brilliant and makes so much sense how that kind of strategy evolved over time on the data piece.

304
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:30,800
It's interesting because you all were operating in a time when, you know, things like RevenueCat didn't exist.

305
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:33,040
Braze was probably just getting off the ground.

