00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:49,760
Talking to your portfolio companies today that are trying to ramp up subscriptions, do you think it's like much easier today or like what, how would you advise folks differently kind of having gone through the slog of, of, of not having tools and things like that available to you at the time?

307
00:27:50,080 --> 00:27:50,880
Yeah, it's a great question.

308
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:55,680
I'll tell you a quick funny story about the lack of tools that we had and the result, the result of that.

309
00:27:56,160 --> 00:28:14,000
We invented a product at some point which was called swipe surge, which, you know, because we observed, we found once we understood the data, we understood that the more people that were on Tinder at any given time, the higher the collective match rate got, meaning it's very logical, but we wanted to prove it out in data.

310
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:19,760
If you're, you know, sitting in, I'm sitting in Los Angeles, if a bunch of people all of a sudden get on Tinder in Los Angeles.

311
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:25,280
everybody's match rate actually goes up collectively because everybody's there swiping actively.

312
00:28:25,360 --> 00:28:29,760
And so, you know, you're seeing more recent matches, you're engaging more, you know, et cetera.

313
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:31,040
So it makes sense.

314
00:28:31,120 --> 00:28:31,760
We saw it in the data.

315
00:28:31,920 --> 00:28:41,600
And then we sort of created this product called SwipeSerge, which would do that on an automatic basis, meaning it would let you know when there was a lot of activity in your area because it was good.

316
00:28:41,920 --> 00:28:48,880
If your goal on Tinder was to match with somebody, then that would increase your odds just by the activity alone would increase your odds.

317
00:28:49,120 --> 00:28:52,000
We didn't have a lot of, you know, products that exist today.

318
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:56,240
And this was a sort of marketing product, sort of hybrid approach.

319
00:28:56,400 --> 00:29:07,200
So I and another guy who's named Jeff Morris, who now has a venture fund and went on, spent a lot of time on revenue product to Tinder and did on went on to do many great things and runs his own venture fund.

320
00:29:07,920 --> 00:29:13,280
Jeff Morris and I set up to sort of initiate Swipe Surge.

321
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:26,640
And by accident, we sent it to everyone, meaning we meant to test it in a geography or two to initiate a test in a geography or two, but we unlocked it somehow across all geographies.

322
00:29:26,800 --> 00:29:34,000
And so, you know, it still worked the way it should, meaning there was Swipe Surge happening, but it went to every geography and we crashed Tinder.

323
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:36,000
It was, we had a...

324
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:44,480
An amazing increase in daily actives as a function of that, but we also accidentally crashed the system and we got a lecture from the OPS team that day.

325
00:29:45,200 --> 00:29:48,560
But anyway, I think had we had more robust tools, these things would have been easier.

326
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:50,560
But, but a, I mean, I guess a successful experiment.

327
00:29:50,960 --> 00:29:52,320
I mean, you can still do that with a robust tool.

328
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:52,880
I've seen it.

329
00:29:52,960 --> 00:29:54,080
So there's no question.

330
00:29:54,240 --> 00:29:59,120
But yeah, we sort of late night in the office one day accidentally went, went rogue.

331
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:02,160
You're not running a consumer product if you haven't DDoSed yourself.

332
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:02,640
That's fair.

333
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:04,000
That's fair enough.

334
00:30:04,080 --> 00:30:07,280
Or you're not running a successful consumer product if you haven't DDoSed yourself.

335
00:30:07,520 --> 00:30:11,280
If your OPS team is not yelling at you every so often, you're probably not pushing hard enough.

336
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:15,360
So, anyway, when I look at our, when I look at companies today, we look at it.

337
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:17,280
We have a bunch of subscription companies in our portfolio.

338
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:19,520
When I look at, you know, I talk to new ones all the time.

339
00:30:20,080 --> 00:30:25,680
Look, I think there was a period of time where I think everybody was trying to turn everything into subscription.

340
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:39,200
And I found myself giving feedback very frequently when I was meeting with founders that like this is just not a subscription, but like subscription is, as you guys have covered in your podcast over and over, it's a beautiful business if you can get it, right?

341
00:30:39,440 --> 00:30:40,240
Like it's beautiful.

342
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:41,760
It's the revenue rolls in.

343
00:30:41,920 --> 00:30:42,480
It's recurring.

344
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:43,520
It's predictable.

345
00:30:43,680 --> 00:30:44,320
It's all the things.

346
00:30:44,640 --> 00:30:46,000
So there was like this over correction.

